    id: 'uc3',
    title: '遗忘也是一种能力',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 12,
    text: 'Your brain does not keep everything that reaches it. Every night, while you sleep, it decides which events to store and which to throw away. Scientists once treated this cleaning as a ___1___ of the memory system, but newer research suggests that forgetting may be just as ___2___ as remembering. In a study in Toronto, researchers trained mice to fear a certain sound. Some of the animals carried a changed gene that made forgetting impossible. A week later, those mice still ___3___ the sound and could not move freely through the room where it had been played. Dropping old information allows the brain to concentrate on what matters now. If you kept every breakfast of your life, the ___4___ of finding the one from yesterday would be terrible. Teachers have long known something close to this. A student who reads a text again every single day often remembers ___5___ than one who waits three days and returns to it, because the effort of ___6___ a half-faded memory makes it stronger. Forgetting also protects our feelings. The embarrassment of a small mistake fades faster than the skill gained that week, and without this slow washing our failures would stay bright forever and make any ___7___ painful. Some researchers therefore describe memory less as a ___8___ that records every event than as a living muscle that grows when it is used and softens when it is left alone. What happens to it is partly under your control. Sleep is the first ___9___, because the cleaning process runs only in the quiet hours. Exercise helps by carrying blood and fuel to the working tissue, and mood matters as well: people who feel safe ___10___ more of what they see. So if one week of study seems to disappear, do not panic. Test yourself, sleep longer, and the missing part is often easier to ___11___ than you expect. There is a social side too. Couples and close friends often remember for each other, a habit so natural that we cannot recite our own phone number or describe the road to a place we visit every day. Scientists call this shared storage, and it frees the mind for new subjects. Understanding forgetting does not make an exam easier, but it changes the way you prepare. Fewer ___12___ readings and more active self-testing, with rest placed in the centre of the plan instead of at the end, will serve you better. The aim is not a perfect record of every day but a mind that holds what is useful and lets the rest ___13___ away. Once you accept that, an empty afternoon stops feeling like waste and starts to feel like part of the ___14___, because a brain that deletes well is not a weak one; it is a busy one deciding where to spend its limited ___15___ on the things you will need next year.',
