    title: '一场辩论赛教会我的事',
    exam: '高考',
    mins: 12,
    text: 'The day I stood up to speak in a school debate, I believed that winning was mostly a matter of talking well. I had prepared forty notes and had practised my opening line until it sounded like a ___1___. My partner, Chen Yu, had filled eleven pages with questions that our opponents might raise. I was the faster speaker, and I asked her to leave half of the work to me. The first round went as I had planned. I spoke for two minutes, and the judges wrote something on their cards. What I did not notice was that the other team had finished a sentence before mine ended, and the hall ___2___ at my timing. By the third round our opponents had stopped answering my arguments and began answering the questions Chen Yu had put on paper the week before. Their speaker was slow and careful, and every point she made carried a ___3___ from our own school life. I felt the ground move under me. In the final round I forgot my opening line, looked down at my notes and found them useless, because I had written speeches rather than ___4___. We came second. Chen Yu said nothing unkind on the bus home, and that silence was worse than any comment. For a week I kept returning to one question: what had she done that I had not? The answer came slowly. She had spent two weeks listening — to our teachers, to younger students, to the school guard who counted bicycles at the gate — and she had written down the exact words people used when they explained why they felt ___5___ about a rule. She treated an argument as a house that needs a floor, and a floor needs the voices of the people who will stand on it. My own notes were only opinions about my own cleverness. In the next term I asked the club to let me help with research instead of speaking, and I learned to build my ___6___ first and my voice afterwards. There is a further lesson that I did not expect. When you listen to someone carefully, that person begins to listen back. A shopkeeper near our school told us how the price of milk had changed and why small shops close one by one, and his numbers were more ___7___ than any chart in our textbook. When we used his words in the final debate, we quoted no famous writer and quoted no report; we quoted a man who had counted boxes for thirty years, and he came to watch us. We came second again. I did not mind. What I had wanted, finally, was never applause or a medal but the right to stand up and say something I could ___8___, and that took longer than any opening line I ever practised. Speaking is the part of thinking that other people can see. If the part below the surface is ___9___, the visible part will shake the moment a real question arrives. My partner never argued with me about that. She simply kept eleven pages beside my forty notes, and let the difference speak. The habit she gave me is smaller than a skill and larger than a prize: before I try to change a ___10___, I ask the people inside it what they see.',
